{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of quiet that exists only at 6 a.m., before the world remembers it is supposed to be loud. The kettle hasn't whistled yet, the cars haven't started their daily parade, and even the birds seem to be clearing their throats politely before beginning their chorus. I love that hour.\n\nIt feels borrowed, like a secret pocket of time that doesn't count. You can sit at the kitchen table with cold hands around a warm mug and just be a person, not a worker, not an inbox, not a to-do list. The light is different then too \u2014 thinner, bluer, as if the sun hasn't fully committed to the day.\n\nI've been thinking a lot about how we try to capture moments like that. We take photos, we write them down, we try to bottle the light. But maybe some moments are meant to evaporate. Like the smell of rain on hot pavement, or the exact way your childhood bedroom felt at night. Their beauty is in their impermanence.\n\nThe Japanese have a word, *komorebi*, for sunlight filtering through leaves. We don't have a single word for 6 a.m. kitchen light, but we feel it anyway. Perhaps that is enough \u2014 to notice it. To sit in it for a minute longer before the day begins to ask things of you. To let the quiet do its quiet work.\n\nAnd when you finally stand up, the day feels just a little softer for having waited for you to begin properly together.",
